-- title:
--   Principal divisors have degree zero for finite separable extensions of K(x)
-- statement:
--   Let $K$ and $F$ be fields with $F$ a $K$-algebra, and let $x \in F$ be transcendental over $K$. Write $K\langle x\rangle =$ `IntermediateField.adjoin K {x}` for the intermediate field generated by $x$. Assume that $F$ is finite-dimensional over $K\langle x\rangle$ and that the extension $F/K\langle x\rangle$ is separable. The conclusion is `HasPrincipalDivisors K F`: for every $f \in F$ with $f \neq 0$ there exists a finitely supported function $D$ from the places of $F/K$ to $\mathbb{Z}$ such that $D(v) = v.\mathrm{ord}(f)$ for every place $v$, and such that the degree of $D$, namely the sum $\sum_v D(v)\cdot v.\mathrm{deg}$ over the support of $D$, vanishes. Here a place of $F/K$ is a valuation subring of $F$ which contains the image of $K$ under the structure map, is not all of $F$, and is a principal ideal ring; the quantities $v.\mathrm{ord}$ and $v.\mathrm{deg}$ are the associated order function and residue degree. Thus, in particular, the family of orders of a nonzero $f$ is asserted to be finitely supported, and the resulting divisor has degree zero.
--
--   This is the statement that in a finite separable extension of a rational function field the divisor of a nonzero function is well defined and of degree zero, in a form free of hypotheses on the characteristic. It is the entry point used to verify that such an $F$ is a curve over $K$ ([`AlgebraicCurve.isCurveOver_of_transcendental`](thm.html#AlgebraicCurve.isCurveOver_of_transcendental)) and, through that, in the divisor-theoretic computations on modular curves.
-- source:
